// Converted from FictionBook Editor 2.7.9: Scripts/21_Поиск и замена/10_Поиск (без замены) по набору регэкспов (версия с исключениями) (заготовка).js
// (converted first from 2.7beta1's "10_Поиск по набору регэкспов (заготовка)", engine version 2.9;
// 2.7.9 made it the template of the version with exceptions, 5.2). A template for a search,
// without replacing anything, by your own list of regular expressions: edit init() below; each
// run starts at the end of the selection (a caret: at the caret, a hit right there included),
// selects the first hit up to the end of the book and shows its description in the status bar;
// nothing found: "Поиск завершен!" in the status bar and a message. Like the original, run()
// returns "Found" or "NotFound" to a script that runs this one (mfbe.scripts.run). As shipped the
// list holds TaKir's macros and one example entry, addRegExp("123", ...), which finds "123".
// The engine is regexp-set.js, the port of the 2.7.9 engine that the group's searches share (one
// identical copy per package; see there).
// HOW TO FILL THE LIST (the original's instructions at the top of init(), translated):
//   addMacros("<name>", "regexp");
//     A named sub-pattern for tagRegExp entries. Allowed in the name: а-я, a-z, "/", "?", "-",
//     "_". Earlier macros may be used in later ones; keep the macros in front of the entries.
//   addRegExp("regexp", "keys", "description", "in which tags", look-behind limit);
//     Backslashes inside the string are doubled; a straight quote takes one backslash: \".
//     "keys" is "" (case-sensitive, the default) or "i" (case-insensitive).
//     "description" is shown in the status bar when the expression finds something.
//     "in which tags" lists "+element" / "-element" separated by spaces: "+poem -stanza +title"
//     searches only paragraphs inside <poem> and <title> but not inside <stanza>. Allowed:
//     section, body, epigraph, cite, poem, stanza, title, subtitle, text-author (no inline
//     elements).
//     The look-behind limit is the longest text a leading (?<= ...) or (?<! ...) looks at: the
//     smaller, the faster; 0, undefined or nothing means no limit.
//     Examples:
//       addRegExp("\\d-\\d", "", "Found: two digits with a hyphen between them.");
//       addRegExp("\"", "", "Found: a straight quote.");
//     To put the caret in front of the found text instead of selecting it, use a look-ahead:
//       addRegExp("(?=\")", "", "Found: a straight quote.");
//     (As in 2.7.9, a hit right at a collapsed caret is not skipped, so such an expression, which
//     matches an empty string, finds the same place again on the next run: move the caret on.)
//     Look-behinds (?<! ...) and (?<= ...) are allowed only at the start of the expression and
//     are checked apart from the rest of it: (?<=а)б|в finds "б or в, preceded by а".
//   tagRegExp("regexp", "keys", "description", "in which tags", look-behind limit);
//     The same, but the expression sees the paragraph's HTML, its inline formatting as tags, and
//     may use the macros: <emphasis>, </emphasis>, <strong>, </strong>, <sup>, </sup>, <sub>,
//     </sub>, <strikethrough>, </strikethrough>, <code>, </code>, <любые-теги> ("any tags") and
//     your own. Example (italics inside a word):
//       tagRegExp("[а-яё]<emphasis>[а-яё]+?</emphasis>|<emphasis>[а-яё]+?</emphasis>[а-яё]", "i", "Found: italics inside a word.");
//   Exceptions (stokber): an entry whose description starts with "Пропустить:" (right after the
//     opening quote, nothing before it) catches a false alarm: its hit is passed over and the
//     search goes on right after it. Put the exceptions at the top, before the main entries. To
//     try a single exception as an ordinary entry, put any character, a space will do, in front
//     of "Пропустить" (the original's other advice, to comment out the line of its engine that
//     tests for "Пропустить", applies to regexp-set.js here).
